<section class="band">
	<div class="wrap split">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">How a change happens</p>
			<h2>You see the change before it happens. You can undo it after.</h2>
			<ol class="steps">
				<li><strong>You ask in plain language.</strong> For example: tag the next 10 items without a topic.</li>
				<li><strong>Sub-Sub prepares the change as a dry run.</strong> The preview shows each item, with the tags it adds in green and the tags it removes in red.</li>
				<li><strong>You select Yes or No.</strong> The check is in the program, not in the instructions to the model. The model cannot skip it.</li>
				<li><strong>The change goes into a journal.</strong> <code>/undo</code> reverts it. Nothing is deleted: items go to the Zotero trash.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>
		<figure class="shot approval-shot">
			<img class="only-light" src="/assets/img/web-approval.webp?v={{ assetVersion }}" width="1200" height="600" loading="lazy" alt="An approval dialog. It asks: Apply tag items? Two items would change. One gets three new topic tags; the other gets one new tag and loses type/rct. Buttons: No and Yes.">
			<img class="only-dark" src="/assets/img/web-approval-dark.webp?v={{ assetVersion }}" width="1200" height="600" loading="lazy" alt="">
		</figure>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap">
		<p class="eyebrow">Two modes</p>
		<h2>One keeps the library in order. The other reads.</h2>
		<div class="modes">
			<article class="mode librarian">
				<h3>Librarian</h3>
				<p class="mode-lead">Changes your library, one approved change at a time.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Tags items with the tags in your own list, <code>Systems/Zotero tags.md</code>. It refuses other tags.</li>
					<li>Writes tag proposals into a review note that you can edit before you apply it.</li>
					<li>Imports by DOI, PMID or ISBN, with the abstract and a citekey.</li>
					<li>Repairs metadata, finds duplicates, flags retractions and attaches open-access PDFs.</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mode-cmds"><code>/tag-batch</code> <code>/clean-tags</code> <code>/import-queue</code> <code>/history</code> <code>/undo</code></p>
			</article>
			<article class="mode researcher">
				<h3>Researcher</h3>
				<p class="mode-lead">Reads your library and the literature. It cannot change the library.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Answers "what do I have on…?" from your library, with citekeys.</li>
					<li>Searches PubMed, Europe PMC and OpenAlex for works that are not in your library.</li>
					<li>Writes literature notes, syntheses and literature reviews with a screening log. Each note says what Sub-Sub read: the full text, the abstract or only the metadata.</li>
					<li>Checks the references of a manuscript with Starbuck, an optional add-on.</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mode-cmds"><code>/lit</code> <code>/lit-note</code> <code>/synthesis</code> <code>/gaps</code> <code>/compare</code> <code>/verify</code></p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap split terminal-split">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">Browser or terminal</p>
			<h2>The same assistant in both.</h2>
			<p>Open Sub-Sub from its shortcut and it opens in your browser. Type <code>subsub</code> and it opens in the terminal. The modes, the profiles and the approvals are the same.</p>
			<p>The browser view is a small server on your own computer. It listens only on 127.0.0.1 and stops 10 minutes after you close the page.</p>
		</div>
